Prove where the crash comes from: stop nesting the run loop.

The engine starts, the GL context stands, the config is read, the frame loop is
reached -- and about four seconds later:

    EXC_BAD_ACCESS, KERN_INVALID_ADDRESS at 0x110
      libdispatch  <- __CFRunLoopRun <- CFRunLoopRunSpecific
      <- UIKit_PumpEvents <- SDL_PumpEventsInternal <- SDL_WaitEventTimeout
      <- PumpEvents() / Frame() / RunGameOrAtlas()      main.cpp:762
      <- SDL_main                                       main.cpp:842
      <- -[SDLUIKitDelegate postFinishLaunch]
      <- __NSFireDelayedPerform <- __CFRunLoopDoTimer <- __CFRunLoopRun

Read it from the bottom: SDL starts SDL_main from a run-loop *timer callback*,
0 A.D. never gives control back, and SDL_PollEvent then enters the very same run
loop a second time from inside that callback (UIKit_PumpEvents calls
CFRunLoopRunInMode). The nesting is the only thing here that no other platform
does.

This patch switches SDL's iOS pump off. It is a measurement, not a fix: with the
pump off no events reach SDL at all, so nothing can be touched -- but if the
crash goes away and the game draws, the diagnosis is settled.

The fix that follows is to turn the loop around: hand the frame to UIKit with
SDL_iPhoneSetAnimationCallback and return from SDL_main, so each frame starts
*from* the run loop instead of being wrapped inside it. That moves every local
of RunGameOrAtlas onto the heap, which is why it is worth knowing first that it
is the right thing to spend that change on.

--- source/main.cpp
+++ source/main.cpp
@@ -742,6 +742,27 @@
 		else if (!InitNonVisual(args))
 			g_Shutdown = ShutdownType::Quit;
 
+#if OS_IOS
+		// SDL starts SDL_main from a run-loop timer callback
+		// (-[SDLUIKitDelegate postFinishLaunch]), so the frame loop below runs
+		// *inside* the CFRunLoop -- and SDL_PollEvent then enters that same run
+		// loop a second time from in there, because UIKit_PumpEvents calls
+		// CFRunLoopRunInMode. A few seconds of that and libdispatch dereferences
+		// a null pointer:
+		//
+		//   EXC_BAD_ACCESS at 0x110 in libdispatch, from __CFRunLoopRun,
+		//   from UIKit_PumpEvents, from PumpEvents() (main.cpp)
+		//
+		// Switching the pump off proves where the crash comes from and lets the
+		// game render; it also means no events at all reach SDL, so nothing can
+		// be touched. The real fix is to turn the loop around -- hand the frame
+		// to UIKit with SDL_iPhoneSetAnimationCallback and return from SDL_main,
+		// so a frame starts *from* the run loop instead of being wrapped in it.
+		// That moves every local below onto the heap, which is why it is a
+		// separate step.
+		SDL_iPhoneSetEventPump(SDL_FALSE);
+#endif
+
 		try
 		{
 			// MSVC doesn't support copy elision in ternary expressions. So we use a lambda instead.
